 code).\n\n### 1. A heading numbered `0` that has items loses its ref and takes section 1's — minor, **verified**\n**Where:** `src/findings.ts`, `scan()`, at the item branch: `if (section === 0 && sawHeading) section = 1` followed by `label = pick(section, false)`.\n\n**What happens:** the new code delays a numbered heading's ref until its first item. That item then forces section 0 to 1, so the heading is filed under label `1` instead of `0`.\n\n**Scenario:** the report `## 0. Intro\\n- a\\n## 1. B\\n- b`:\n- **Old refs:** `0`, `1.1`, `1`. The heading `0. Intro` kept the ref `0`, and `1: B` named the second heading.\n- **New refs:** `1`, `1.1`, `1/2`, `1/2.1`. The heading `0. Intro` now owns `1`, and the real `## 1. B` section is pushed to `1/2`.\n\n**Consequences:**\n- A decision stored earlier as `0: Intro` now gets a \"no finding\" warning.\n- A stored `1: B` now sorts and resolves against the Intro heading.\n- The same heading with no items under it still gets `0` (`## 0. Intro\\n\\n## 1. B\\n- b` gives `0`, `1`, `1.1`), so the ref depends on whether items follow.\n\nThe old code also collided here (both items were `1.1`), so this only affects reports that number a section 0. A possible fix is to keep the heading's own number for the pending heading row instead of the bumped section.\n\n### Checked and found sound (from reading, plus the passing tests)\n- **Re-run de-duplication** (`newRecords` and `applyBatch`):\n  - It compares against the latest decision per (review, finding), so a decision that a later one reversed is written again.\n  - It is scoped per review, and the complete marker is handled.\n  - Two concurrent runs could still both write, as before this change.\n- **Write cap:** counted in UTF-8 bytes and checked before any write.\n- **`resolveBy` refactor:** it is checked in the same order as the code it replaces.\n- **`config-edit` in lint:** it is shown only when signals are available, matching `add`.\n- **Template omitting headings that have items:** a decision can still name the heading's ref (it remains in `findingRefs`).\n- **Idempotent labelling:** running on its own output changes nothing, including `1/2.N` labels and labels past the 999-repeat cap.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
