eady holds the same bare number. With `- loose`, `- x`, `## 1. Races`, `- lock`, the heading row `1` is dropped, and `findingRefs` has no separate heading entry for it. The bare `1` then resolves to the loose item, so a decision `1: Races` is recorded against the wrong finding. The author lists the collision as a risk, but the notes' claim is wrong for that case.\n\n2. **idem-rerun: the \"(S already recorded)\" count mixes units** (**read**, confirmed by the batch test expectation `recorded 0 decisions on 0 commits (4 already recorded)` for two commits). `applyBatch` sums `skipped` across commits, and it counts the complete marker as a skipped record. `decisions` is the per-commit maximum and excludes the marker. The README says \"S already recorded\" with no unit. The author's risk list covers only the decision count being a maximum, not this sum.\n\n3. **idem-rerun notes: \"re-running is safe\" overstates it** (**read**). Identity includes `by`, and a skip happens only when a record equals the latest decision on that finding. If another arbiter decided the finding after the file was first run, a re-run of the old file writes the old decision again and becomes the latest. That silently reverts a later decision. The single-call reversal test shows this is intended for the same mechanism, but the cross-arbiter case is not listed in the risks, and the README and skill text say re-running is safe without qualification.\n\n4. **config-edit and resolve-by notes check out** (**read**). `lint` reports `config-edit` only when `tryAnalyze` succeeds, which matches the rating-check skip described in the README. The `resolveBy`/`prepareBatch` order of checks matches the old code. The \"same note add prints\" claim holds, since `ratingProblems` uses the same `CONFIG_EDIT_NOTE`.\n\n5. **dup-refs corpus claim is accurate** (**verified**). I ran the 15 corpus cases against `HEAD~1:src/findings.ts` (pre-change) and the new file. Both reproduce `labelled` and `refs` with zero diffs. The corpus is new in this commit, so \"checked-in from the previous version\" means it was generated by the old labeller, which is what the run confirms.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n- **Re-run overrides a later decision by someone else** (see item 3). Not in the author's risks.\n- **Heading row loss past 999 is not the only silent loss.** A run of 1005 empty `## 1. S` headings gives 999 template rows (**verified**). The rows past the cap are dropped silently, with no warning in the template or `--decide-file`. The TODO says this, but no user-facing message does.\n- **Test weakening.** In `every template row is recognised…` the filter `refs = all.filter(r => !all.some(o => o.startsWith(r + '.')))` recreates the implementation's rule, so the test only checks the rule against itself. The new template test covers the behaviour, but this older test no longer checks the template against findingRefs independently (**read**).\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n- `refOf`'s `digits` helper returns `-1` as a sentinel, and the later code then reads `finding[-1]` (**read**). A `null` return would be clearer. Behaviour is correct as tested.\n- `findingTexts` is used only by the batch template (**verified** by grep), so the `hasItems` filter could live in `template()`. Not required.\n\n**Verification performed**\n- Corpus comparison against the pre-change labeller: 15/15 match (verified).\n- Pure probes of `findingTexts`, `findingRefs` and `refOf` on loose, empty, and duplicate-heading reports (verified, outputs as above).\n- I did not run the batch or lint test files. The orchestrator's record covers them, and I did not re-run them.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
