tly the refs it had. That does not hold when a list-less numbered heading shares its number with a loose item. I ran the frozen v0.6 labeller (`test/fixtures/findings-v0.6.ts`) against the new `src/findings.ts`:\n\n- `\"- loose\\n## 1. Empty\"`: old `findingRefs` = `{1}`, new = `{1, 1/2}`. The new labeller adds a `1/2` heading row that the template now offers.\n- `\"- loose\\n- two\\n## 1. Empty\"`: old `{1, 2}`, new `{1, 2, 1/2}`.\n\nThe printed item labels are unchanged in both cases. Only the heading refs differ, and the heading gains a row. The group's property test excludes exactly this shape (`!heads.some(n => n >= 1 && n <= loose)`), so the claim is never checked for it. The behaviour may be the intended fix, since the test named \"a heading without items next to a loose ref `1` takes a free label\" asserts it. But the README sentence \"every report whose old refs were all distinct keeps exactly the refs it had\" is wrong for this case. Either narrow that sentence or include this shape in the comparison.\n\n**`dup-refs`, minor: read.** The `scan` comment says \"a heading ref and an item ref never collide\", but the loose-item case above is a heading ref colliding with a loose item ref. The comment's \"never\" is true only within sections. Correct the comment to match.\n\n### 2. Misgrading and removal candidates\n\nSkipped by the brief's rules.\n\n### 3. Simplifications\n\n**read:** `flush` and the inline items-path heading add duplicate the same logic with different probe kinds (`pick(num, true)` in `flush`, `pick(section, false)` on the items path). This is hard to follow. One helper that takes the probe kind would make the 0-heading and same-section cases easier to read.\n\n### 4. Missing risks\n\n**read, not verified:** `src/html/data.ts:125` passes `labelFindings` output to the HTML page. The new `**[1/2.1]**` label form is not matched by any other pattern I looked at in `src/`, but I did not inspect the page's client-side JS for a `**[N.N]**` regex. Check the HTML view with a `1/2` report before relying on it.\n\n**read:** `applyBatch` calls `readDecisions(sha)` once per commit. Any silently skipped or malformed line makes \"latest\" fall back to an older decision, so a re-run can write a duplicate. The author lists this as a TODO. Confirmed as a real gap, not a claim I could check here.\n\n**read:** The byte cap in `prepareBatch` counts `JSON.stringify(r) + 1` per record. It does not count the actual on-disk serialization from `addReviews`. Any difference in the line format would make the cap inexact. The test with multibyte text suggests the count is right for the current format, but this was not compared against `addReviews` directly.\n\n### Verdict reasoning\n\nThe main issue is the README/group claim that all-distinct reports keep their refs, which the loose-plus-empty-heading case contradicts. The other claims I checked hold: the template filter works by row, the lint `config-edit` note is wired, `resolveBy` keeps the check order, and the remaining TODOs and risks are disclosed.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
