ar without any warning. Group `dup-refs`, `findings-tests` (hostile test).\n\n- **The hostile test is vacuous. Read.** `test/findings.test.ts` asserts `new Set(refs).size === refs.length`, but `findingRefs` returns a `Set`, so that holds by construction. The test does not check the uniqueness it names. Group `findings-tests`.\n\n- **Existing decisions can change meaning for reports with a same-number section that is not a second `1.` heading. Verified.** The author's claim that refs of reports \"without repeats are byte-identical\" is true only for reports without any repeat. In the old code, an unnumbered heading before `## 1. Races` gave both sections number 1, so their items collided on `1.x`. The new code gives `## Summary` the ref `1.1` and `## 1. Races` the refs `1b.1`, `1b.2` and so on. A decision already recorded against `1.2`, meaning Races' second item, now warns and no longer points at that item. The same applies to an empty `## 1.` heading followed by a second `## 1.`. This is a compatibility risk for in-flight reviews, and the risk list omits it. Groups `dup-refs`, `idem-rerun` (recorded decisions name refs).\n\n## 2. Misgraded necessity\nSkipped per the review rules.\n\n## 3. Removal candidates\nSkipped per the review rules.\n\n## 4. Simplifications\n- **Read.** `refOf` could keep the old strict form (no letters) and accept suffixes only when they match a suffix that `labelFindings` actually emitted. That would stop `2nd:` and `1st)` from becoming refs, and it avoids the ordinal change above.\n- **Read.** The `refKey` regex and the `compareKeys` suffix-length comparison are fine as written. No change needed there.\n\n## 5. Missing risks\n- The ordinal-as-ref change in item 1, and the lost-rows behaviour past 701 repeats in item 2 (both **verified**).\n- Existing reviews whose refs change under the new numbering (item 4), which affects recorded decisions.\n- **Read.** `applyBatch` now calls `readDecisions` once per commit before writing, which adds a git read for each commit in a range. This is a small cost, not a defect.\n- **Read.** Group `todo-done` replaces the six items with two follow-ups. The remaining \"two parsers\" item is accurate. I did not find a mismatch in the TODO text.\n\nI checked the other claims against the diff and found no mismatches: the `resolveBy` order of checks, the cap in UTF-8 bytes (the non-ASCII test fixture is in fact over 512 KiB in bytes and under it in units), the idempotency key, the suffix function (`b`…`z`, `aa`…`zz`, `zz` at 701), and the ordering of `1b` before `1b.1` before `2`.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
