s any ref in the report. It draws the \"no finding\" warning and sorts against the wrong finding.\n   - **Docs:** the README says reports \"without such a collision\" keep their refs. That holds only under the new definition of collision, not for every report that was unambiguous before. The shape is rare: section numbers have to go backwards after a heading with no list under it.\n   - **How I checked:** I ran `findingRefs` and `findingTexts` on it in a scratch script and got `['2','1','1.1','2/2.1']`.\n\n2. **Minor, test doesn't test what it claims** (`test/batch.test.ts`, the test \"every template row is recognised as its report ref\"; **read**)\n   - The new `headings` column is `[]` for every report, so heading-only template rows are never exercised there.\n   - The expected refs now come from `labelFindings(report)`, the code under test, instead of an independent source. If the labeller and the template both drift the same way, the test still passes.\n   - Its sort key splits only on `.`, so a `1/2` ref would sort as `NaN` if a fixture ever had one.\n   - Heading rows are covered elsewhere (the \"no row for a numbered heading with items\" test), so this is a loss of independence, not of coverage.\n\nThings I checked that came out clean:\n- **Speed on hostile input (verified):** a scan of a maximum-size report (256 KiB of findings) takes about 1.9 s for 32,000 empty repeated headings and about 1.2 s for 20,000 headings with one item each. `--decide-template` scans twice, so a hostile report costs a few seconds. That's bounded, so I'm not counting it as a defect.\n- **Read only:**\n  - The \"already recorded\" check works per review and per finding text, and `readDecisions` ignores repeated note lines.\n  - The write cap counts bytes, as the JSON lines are written.\n  - The complete marker gets its own key.\n  - The heading `0` path flushes correctly.\n  - In my scratch runs, the heading-clash warning fired for a loose item `1` next to a numbered heading `1`.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
