wnRef` calls `findingRefs(report.findings)` once per entry (`src/commands/reviews.ts:87`), so each entry re-scans the report. A 3-entry file took **16.2 s**. Extrapolated, a 500-entry file, which the batch limit allows, would take roughly 40 minutes.\n- The included hostile test only uses 3,000 sections, against a 3 s limit, so it doesn't catch this.\n\n**Fix:** keep the next free repeat number for each section number instead of probing from 1 every time, and scan the report once per batch rather than once per entry.\n\n### 2. Minor: `--decide-template` warns about ambiguous refs when nothing is ambiguous (verified at function level)\n\n**Where:** `src/findings.ts`, `add()`, which records a clash for any heading whose ref is already taken, and `src/commands/reviews.ts`, where the `findingClashes` warning is printed.\n\n**Scenario:** the report `## 1. Empty` / `## 1. B` / `- b`. This is the corpus case `emptyThenSame`, which the change is meant to leave as it was.\n- `findingTexts` gives the rows `[\"1\", \"1.1\"]`, so the template lists every finding.\n- `findingClashes` still returns `[\"1\"]`. The template command therefore prints \"these refs are ambiguous, so the template cannot list every finding under its own: 1\" on stderr.\n- The second heading has items, so it would never be a template row. The warning claims a lost row that doesn't exist.\n\n**Fix:** record a clash only when the heading that lost its ref has no items.\n\n### Checked and found fine\n- **Re-run skipping:** `newRecords` keys on review and finding, compares against the latest decision only, and includes `by`.\n- **Write cap:** the 512 KiB limit counts bytes, and nothing is written when it is exceeded.\n- **lint:** the `config-edit` note is reported.\n- **Ordering:** `refOf` and `orderDecisions` handle the new `1/2` refs.\n\nI made no code changes, so there was nothing to gloss.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
