b48da8241059d79c","eab14b56483283eb","09bd652341a96d42","9a1f3bffdb9409b3","2c236d7e10513002","1119e7204233f5b6","de789b6376b3ebdf","a0dfd09eecc05ac4","dc27b22c59e8b4a9","60a72a3082c84045","8222daec271f8e6f","44dad076011e62c1","eb12574abea1749d"],"findings":"No suggestions were applied or tested in the clone, and I ran no tests. Every point below is **read**. Line numbers are from the clone's `src/findings.ts`, `src/commands/reviews.ts` and `src/batch.ts`.\n\n1. **`findings-suffix-table`: replace the 701-entry `SUFFIXES` array with a small function (`findings.ts:40-43`).** The array is built at load time, and `sectionLabel` clamps with `Math.min`. A bijective base-26 function would produce the same `b`…`z`, `aa`…`zz` sequence with no table, and the clamp could become an explicit cap. Benefit: fewer concepts for the next reader. Size: about 5 lines. Check: the existing \"701 repeats\" hostile test and the `orderDecisions` test would confirm equivalence.\n\n2. **`findings-items-tracking`: drop the `headingRefs`/`withItems` pair and the final intersection (`findings.ts:152-153, 168, 180, 185`).** The scan records every heading label in `headingRefs`, records every item-bearing label in `withItems`, then intersects them. Tracking the currently open numbered heading (set on a numbered heading, cleared on any heading, and added to `headingsWithItems` when an item appears) gives the same set in one structure. Benefit: fewer parallel sets and one fewer step to reason about. Size: small, about 8 lines inside `scan`.\n\n3. **`stripLabel` has two parsers (`findings.ts`, the `stripLabel` function).** The change adds a regex branch for suffixed labels in front of the existing manual character loop, so one label format is parsed two ways. A single regex covering `\\d{1,3}[a-z]{0,2}` and the dotted item part would replace both. Benefit: one parser to keep correct. Size: moderate. This needs the old 20-character limit and the `**[1.2]**` cases checked before it is safe, so it is unverified.\n\n4. **`compareRefs` (`findings.ts:231` onward) builds an object and regex per comparison.** It is correct, but a sort over many refs parses every ref on every comparison, and the non-null assertion `!` depends on the earlier regex. Parsing once into a key, or keeping the old split logic and only adding the suffix comparison, would be shorter. Benefit: smaller and cheaper. Size: small. The cost is negligible at realistic sizes (hundreds of refs), so this is about clarity more than performance.\n\n5. **`--decide-file` counting (`reviews.ts:110-115`).** `n` and `marked` are recomputed by filtering `records` against the `fresh` set after the loop. Counting written records inside the loop, or checking `todo.some((r) => r.complete)`, would remove the index-based `fresh.has(records[i])` lookups. Benefit: less indirection in the command handler. Size: small.\n\nNot raised: `prepareBatch` and `resolveBy` are a clean reuse of logic that was duplicated across three paths, so I'd keep them as they are.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
