3","f33b6d2f8c17e5ae","f455d8586d1e70be","f4a7cc67d1f49b9b","f4dcbfd5b964984c","f4edbf7023903a1f","f5dff68dabff3d9a","f6a695af22c556f9","f6ee9f140e3582c5","f704d87795f6b8c7","f7ec2f549ba2ce19","f8396d5876ed3505","f8c3360e50d5394f","f9169d4a16ec796f","f95c127be0a81528","f99485ff0b7aea31","f9c7c1ebc54d0bc9","fc59553258d707ef","fcf4e06972a63ffb","fd347bfb6c0bfa59","ff79a37c186f9a5a","ff7ee56faf3f15d8"],"groups":["5c0d6e8dc9893440","6a63ad11ef5c0d0c","d436af5e55856807","65ed6282bca3fa6a","7c9510cb8bc15e4d","29a6af1a65f5fc8b","09bd652341a96d42","9a1f3bffdb9409b3","2c236d7e10513002","1119e7204233f5b6","de789b6376b3ebdf","08a7c130929b2633","dc27b22c59e8b4a9","bdcdaf4d558eb04f","8222daec271f8e6f","3a38bc0b3593fcbd","f0075f85d0742491","eb12574abea1749d"],"findings":"I found four defects, all **verified**: two major and two minor. I ran each one on the clone's own CLI (`bin/git-gloss.js`) or directly against `src/findings.ts`, and compared with the pre-change code at HEAD~1 in a temporary worktree.\n\n### 1. An unnumbered heading or bold line now shifts refs in reports that never repeat a number — major, verified\n`src/findings.ts:172` (`else { section += 1; label = enter(section) }`)\n\nAn unnumbered heading, or a bold-only line such as `**Details**`, after a numbered section uses up the next section number. A later real `## 2.` heading then counts as a repeat and gets a `b` suffix.\n- **Input:** a report reading `## 1. Bugs` / `- a` / `**Details**` / `## 2. Style` / `- b`.\n- **Before:** the item under `## 2. Style` was `[2.1]` and the heading was `2`.\n- **Now:** the item is `[2b.1]` and the heading is `2b`, so the report has no `2` or `2.1` ref at all.\n- **Effect:** a decision `--finding \"2.1 b\"` now warns `has no finding 2.1`. Decisions already recorded against the old refs no longer match the labels `reviews` shows (`fixed: 2.1 b` under an item labelled `[2b.1]`). This contradicts the README's claim that \"refs of reports without repeats are unchanged\". Bold-only lines between numbered sections are common in reviewer output.\n\n### 2. The template drops a finding that sits above the first heading — major, verified\n`src/findings.ts:204` (`findingTexts` filter)\n\nItems before the first heading get the bare refs `1`, `2`, and so on. A later numbered heading `## 1.` with items adds the label `1` to `headingsWithItems`. That filter then removes the row for the *item* `1`, which is a real finding, not a heading.\n- **Input:** `- loose problem` / `- another` / `## 1. Races` / `- lock`.\n- **Before:** `findingTexts` gave `[1 loose problem], [2 another], [1.1 lock]`.\n- **Now:** it gives `[2 another], [1.1 lock]`.\n- **Effect:** \"loose problem\" gets no template row, so an arbiter filling in the template never decides it.\n\n### 3. `refOf` now reads ordinary words as refs — minor, verified\n`src/findings.ts:214-227`\n\nAny digits followed by one or two lowercase letters and then `:`, `)` or `. ` now parse as a ref: `2fa: bypass` → `2fa`, `1st. pass` → `1st`, `2nd) x` → `2nd`, `3d: render` → `3d`. All of these returned `null` before.\n- **Effect:** `--decide --finding \"2fa: bypass\"` now prints a misleading `has no finding 2fa ... recording it anyway` warning. `orderDecisions` also sorts these free-text decisions into the ref-ordered block instead of the time-ordered rest.\n\n### 4. Re-running a file can silently drop a reversed decision — minor, verified\n`src/batch.ts:131-137` (`newRecords` / `sameDecision`)\n\nThe duplicate check ignores time, so a decision that was already made once is never recorded again, even after a later decision overrode it.\n- **Steps:** record `1.1 a: fixed` from a file, then `--decide --finding \"1.1 a\" --outcome rejected`, then re-run the same file to go back to `fixed`.\n- **Output:** `recorded 0 decisions on 0 commits (1 already recorded)`.\n- **Effect:** the timeline ends at `rejected`, which is not what the arbiter just decided.\n\nNot reproduced: the 512 KiB write cap, the range fill-in, the review-id scoping in `sameDecision`, the `lint` config-edit note, and the suffix arithmetic (`b`…`z`, `aa`…`zz`, capped at 701) all behaved correctly on reading. I didn't run the repo's test files.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
